me. If I pass, I force the Landlord to play something higher than a 9. If the Landlord plays, I get more information about his hand.\n\nSo, strategically, the move is clear: Pass. The JSON object for pass is `{"cards": []}`.\n\nI have triple-checked everything. I am confident in this play.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate played a ♠9 to beat the Landlord\'s ♦6. As the peasant sitting immediately before the Landlord ("Gatekeeper" position), my primary role is to block the Landlord. Since the current winning card belongs to my teammate, I should not interfere. Beating the 9 with a 10 or J would likely help the Landlord by bridging the gap to higher cards (Q/K). Beating it with a high card (A or 2) would waste my strong control cards (AA, 222) which are better saved to counter the Landlord\'s high plays. Passing puts pressure on the Landlord to spend a higher card to regain the lead.\n\nReason: Helping the teammate implies not beating their cards unless necessary to save them or if I can definitely clear my hand. The 9 is a mid-range card; forcing the Landlord to beat it or pass is advantageous. My hand is top-heavy (KK, AA, 222), so I am well-equipped to punish the Landlord if they play high cards over the 9.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
